\section1 Scenes

First, you will need to create the following 3 scenes:
\list
    \li \c MainMenuScene
    \li \c DungeonScene
    \li \c GameOverScene
\endlist

\section3 MenuButton

For the \c MainMenu and \c GameOverScene we will use the same \c MenuButton. \br
Start with creating the \c ui subfolder inside the \c qml directory. All upcoming folder will be placed inside the \c qml directory.\br
Now create a new file inside the \c ui subfolder and copy this code.\br
\c ui/MenuButton.qml
\quotefile MenuButton.qml
\br

\section3 MainMenuScene

Then, create the \c MainMenuScene, which only includes the background, banner and a play button that switches the scene to the DungeonScene.\br
\c scenes/MainMenuScene.qml
\quotefile MainMenuScene.qml
\br

\section3 DungeonScene and GameOverScene

Next, create your DungeonScene and GameOverScene.\br
\c scenes/DungeonScene.qml
\code QML
import QtQuick
import Felgo

import "../ui"

Scene {
    id: dungeonScene

    property int currentDungeonLevel: 1

    Rectangle {
        id: background
        anchors.fill: parent
        color: "#101010"
    }
}

\endcode
\br
\c scenes/GameOverScene.qml
\quotefile GameOverScene.qml
\br

\section3 Scene Integration

These scenes now need to be integrated and connected, and added to the \c Main.qml file. \br
\c Main.qml
\quotefile Main.qml
\br

Here in the \c Main.qml you can also see the \c FontLoader, which allows you to load a custom font, such as the \c PixelOperator8 font we will use.\br

Now when you start the game you will be able to see the \c MainMenuScene and can switch to the \c DungeonScene, which is still empty.\br
We will connect the \c GameOverScene later, but if you want to see it now, set its visible property to true.\br

